[Music]  welcome back to cbs this morning  saturday the sport of skateboarding made  its olympic debut at the games now  underway  in tokyo and japanese athletes have been  dominant winning 5 out of 12 medals  including three gold and as lucy kraft  reports it's a testament to how the  sport  has overcome being stigmatized in japan  meet shimon iwazawa japan's  skateboarding evangelist  a youtuber with over 200 000 subscribers  he test drives skateparks  demystifies new tricks and most of all  tirelessly preaches the gospel of  skating  the star turns are 180 for shimon a  self-described  misfit who briefly dropped out of school  he found  unlikely salvation on a narrow plank of  wood and the friendship of fellow  outcasts back then skateboarders were  more anti-social people that couldn't  fit  in society like like me like i couldn't  fit in school i really felt like i  didn't have freedom  but skateboarding gave me freedom now 23  skateboarding has taken shimon on skate  aid trips bringing boards to  underprivileged kids in sri lanka  he's got his own clothing line  crowdfunds for skaters trying to go pro  and has just published a memoir  skateboarding gave me a place to be  and a future the urban jungle of  japanese cities packed with rails ledges  and slopes  has proven an irresistible playground  for tourists  nowadays these public streets are a  skating no-go zone  among many japanese skateboarding still  carries the whiff of delinquency  if i'm holding my skateboard and just  walking some people  will tell me like what are you doing  that's for losers  or like you're not supposed to be doing  that and yet  despite a lack of space and sympathy  japanese skaters have  excelled at a sport that levels the  playing field for athletes with small  light physiques and exploits the  country's strength at mastering detailed  maneuvers  thanks in part to skateboarding's  olympic visibility shimon said the sport  is finally  starting to shed its outsider status  japanese society has a lot of rules a  lot of restrictions and people are  really getting tired of this  skateboarding is a sport of freedom  people are just really enjoying the  freedom of it  for cbs this morning saturday lucy craft  tokyo i have to tell you guys i covered  when i  used to be in my only sport world i  covered the x games and skateboarding  and how it was like this  rebellious and a rebel kind of sport and  i love  seeing it now on the olympic stage and  seeing the impact in japan  too as well i was watching some of the  biking the other day the bmx stuff and  it's  incredible the rock climbing i mean the  the the climbing  because i love to climb and i don't get  to do it as much as i i've done it in  the past but it is  you know it's it's gotten to the point  now where we're gonna have so  many sports in the olympics i'm  wondering we're gonna have to expand the  three weeks  something something you've seen it in  ping pong they're like toweling off  because it's such a it's really a  grueling workout yeah i don't play like  that yeah and you're like  10 feet away from the table i love it  that'll be our next thing ping pong  competition

